Yes, the core idea is sound, but I would broaden it from **“everything runs through the index”** to:

> **TemplarOS should prohibit polling for local state whenever an authoritative event source exists. Services should normally sleep or not exist at all until the state they depend on changes.**

The file index then becomes one major **state authority**, not the authority for the whole machine.

The reason for that distinction is that your basic observation is almost right, but “disk or RAM changed” is too low-level to be useful. A service might care about:

* a file appearing;
* a USB device connecting;
* Wi-Fi losing its link;
* memory pressure crossing a threshold;
* a process exiting;
* the battery reaching 10%;
* a new display connecting;
* a network packet arriving;
* a D-Bus property changing;
* a remote server changing;
* a certificate becoming old;
* 6 PM arriving.

They all ultimately involve memory somewhere, but monitoring RAM writes would be vastly more expensive than polling. Linux already exposes higher-level event interfaces for most of them.

## The architecture I would use

Think of TemplarOS as having **event authorities**:

```text
                 TemplarOS state changes
                         │
       ┌─────────────────┼──────────────────┐
       │                 │                  │
 filesystem         hardware/device      resources
       │                 │                  │
 unified index         udev             PSI/cgroups
       │                 │                  │
       └─────────────┬───┴──────────────────┘
                     │
              kernel/network
                     │
                  netlink
                     │
              applications
                     │
                D-Bus/socket
                     │
             time itself changes
                     │
              systemd timers
```

Services subscribe to the **highest-level authoritative source** appropriate to them.

Linux is already built to support this kind of design. Netlink provides asynchronous kernel notifications, fanotify provides filesystem event notification, and PSI can literally be configured so a process blocks in `poll()` until CPU/memory/I/O pressure crosses a chosen threshold. ([man7.org][1])

That's much better than:

```text
while true {
    inspect_world();
    sleep(5 seconds);
}
```

---

# The file index can eliminate a lot of polling

This part of your idea is particularly strong.

Suppose something currently does:

```text
every 10 seconds:
    scan ~/Downloads
    determine whether anything appeared
```

That should absolutely disappear.

The unified index already knows:

```text
generation 8291:
    CREATE ~/Downloads/foo.pdf
```

So the service can express:

```text
I care about:
    path under ~/Downloads
    type = file
    maybe extension = pdf
```

and remain asleep otherwise.

Or something checks whether a Git repository gained/removed files.

Same answer.

Or Silvaview repeatedly scans directories looking for storage changes.

Again, don't do that.

The index already maintains the namespace and relevant metadata.

This gives you a useful rule:

> **No Templar component may independently rescan a filesystem namespace already represented by the shared index unless it is repairing/verifying that index.**

That is worth making an architectural invariant.

---

# But don't have every service literally query SQLite

That's how the idea could go wrong.

You don't want:

```text
                    index.sqlite
                   /     |      \
                  /      |       \
           resourced   shell    updater
              |          |         |
             ...        ...       ...
```

with random processes treating the index database as shared memory.

Instead:

```text
filesystem
    ↓
lege-filed / index owner
    ↓
persistent indexed state
    ↓
typed API
    ├── Search(...)
    ├── ChangesSince(generation)
    ├── Subscribe(...)
    └── GetState(...)
```

Only the index owner touches its database.

Consumers depend on its **API and generations**, not its SQLite representation.

That lets you change SQLite, FTS, schemas, or even the entire backing implementation later.

---

# There is an even stronger idea hiding here: state + events

A raw event stream isn't enough.

Consider:

```text
service sleeps
file changes
event emitted
service launches later
```

If it depended purely on seeing the event live, it missed it.

Your index architecture already solves this elegantly through generations:

```text
Service last handled generation: 8300

Current generation:              8341

ChangesSince(8300)
        ↓
41 changes
```

That means a service doesn't need to remain running merely to avoid missing something.

This is extremely valuable.

I'd make this a general Templar principle:

> **Whenever practical, event sources should have recoverable state or sequence numbers so consumers do not have to remain resident simply to observe events.**

For files, that's your indexed generations.

For other subsystems, the domain owner often already maintains the current state.

So a service can do:

```text
wake
↓
ask current state/change sequence
↓
act
↓
exit
```

rather than live forever.

---

# Then systemd becomes useful rather than something to fight

TemplarOS already inherits an excellent activation system.

D-Bus services can be launched on demand rather than running for the whole session, and systemd can manage that activation. ([D-Bus][2])

So instead of:

```text
boot
 ↓
launch 27 Templar daemons
 ↓
27 mostly idle processes forever
```

aim for:

```text
boot
 ↓
5 genuinely persistent system components
 ↓
everything else dormant
```

Then:

```text
client calls Files API
       ↓
D-Bus activation
       ↓
service starts
       ↓
does work
       ↓
becomes idle
       ↓
exits
```

Or socket activation:

```text
kernel/systemd owns socket
       ↓
nothing running
       ↓
connection arrives
       ↓
service starts
```

D-Bus explicitly supports on-demand service activation, including delegating activation to systemd. ([D-Bus][2])

This is very aligned with what you're trying to achieve.

---

# I would establish a TemplarOS hierarchy of observation

Every service needing to know about state should be forced down this decision tree:

```text
1. Is there already an authoritative owner?
       YES → subscribe/query it.

2. Does the kernel expose an event FD?
       YES → poll/epoll that FD.

3. Does another system service emit a D-Bus/netlink event?
       YES → subscribe/use activation.

4. Is the condition inherently time-based?
       YES → systemd timer.

5. Is the underlying event mechanism lossy?
       YES → event-driven operation
             + infrequent reconciliation.

6. Nothing exposes the change?
       → polling allowed as explicit exception.
```

Polling becomes the **last resort**, rather than the default programming technique.

---

# Examples

### Filesystem

Bad:

```text
every 5 seconds:
    stat 20,000 files
```

Templar:

```text
filesystem event
      ↓
shared index
      ↓
generation advances
      ↓
interested operation runs
```

---

### Network

Don't put this through the file index.

Use NetworkManager/netlink.

Linux netlink is explicitly designed for asynchronous kernel→userspace notifications, including network link/address/routing state. ([man7.org][3])

So:

```text
Wi-Fi link changes
      ↓
kernel/netlink
      ↓
NetworkManager
      ↓
D-Bus signal
      ↓
Templar shell reacts
```

No:

```text
every 1 second:
    ask ifconfig whether Wi-Fi changed
```

---

### Hardware

Use udev.

`udevadm monitor` is literally an event listener for kernel and udev device events. ([freedesktop.org][4])

```text
USB disk inserted
      ↓
kernel uevent
      ↓
udev
      ↓
filed
      ↓
mount/index/update UI
```

No removable-drive poller.

---

### Resource manager

This one is especially relevant to TemplarOS.

Bad:

```text
every 250 ms:
    read CPU
    read memory
    read IO
    decide whether system is overloaded
```

Linux PSI lets you register a pressure threshold and then block on `poll()`/`epoll()` until that threshold is crossed. ([Linux Kernel Archives][5])

So `resourced` can potentially do:

```text
sleep indefinitely
        ↓
memory pressure becomes significant
        ↓
kernel wakes resourced
        ↓
throttle/pause low-value compilation
```

That is almost exactly your philosophy implemented by the kernel.

---

# RAM should not have a general "index"

This is the one part I would reject.

An index of filesystem state works because filesystem changes are:

* relatively infrequent;
* semantically meaningful;
* persistent;
* naturally named/identified.

RAM writes are:

* continuous;
* enormous in volume;
* usually meaningless in isolation.

During a Rust compilation your CPU might generate billions of memory operations.

Trying to turn those into a state index would destroy performance.

Instead, monitor the **semantic abstraction produced by the kernel**:

```text
RAM writes
    ↓
kernel MM
    ↓
memory pressure / cgroup state / OOM conditions
    ↓
PSI event
```

The insight is therefore not:

> observe every change.

It's:

> **observe every meaningful transition at the highest layer that already knows what it means.**

That's much cheaper.

---

# Time shouldn't be banned either

There are legitimate time-based conditions.

Examples:

```text
Run a backup at midnight.
Certificate expires in 7 days.
Retry failed server connection in 30 seconds.
Refresh remote repository metadata after 6 hours.
Garbage collect cache once a week.
```

Nothing changes on the local filesystem to announce that:

> this certificate is now one day closer to expiration.

Time itself is the state transition.

So systemd timers should remain allowed.

The rule I'd use is:

> **Time may trigger something only when elapsed/calendar time is itself part of the condition—not as a substitute for observing another condition.**

Good:

```text
OnCalendar=weekly
    → scrub data drive
```

Bad:

```text
OnUnitActiveSec=5s
    → check whether network changed
```

That's a useful distinction.

---

# Another legitimate exception: reconciliation

Event systems aren't magically perfect.

We already encountered this with filesystem events:

* queues overflow;
* daemon may be stopped;
* external changes may occur;
* bugs happen.

So there is a useful pattern:

```text
            EVENT PATH
               │
          normal operation
               │
               ↓
          near-zero overhead


        RECONCILIATION PATH
               │
        rarely / on evidence
               │
               ↓
          verify reality
```

You shouldn't make the philosophical mistake of saying:

> polling is prohibited, therefore reconciliation is prohibited.

Instead:

> **continuous polling is prohibited where event-driven observation is available.**

Occasional verification is reliability engineering.

Your Btrfs/index-delta work is a very good example: it makes reconciliation proportional to changes rather than repeatedly scanning everything.

---

# I wouldn't build a giant new Templar event daemon

There's a temptation now to create:

```text
templar-eventd
```

that everything connects to.

I wouldn't.

Linux/systemd already gives you most of the transport:

* D-Bus;
* Unix sockets;
* epoll;
* netlink;
* udev;
* fanotify;
* pidfds;
* PSI;
* cgroups;
* systemd activation.

Adding another always-running broker to eliminate always-running services would be slightly self-defeating.

Instead, create a **common Rust library and protocol conventions**.

For example:

```text
crates/templar-events/
```

could expose ergonomic wrappers around:

```rust
FilesystemChanges
DeviceChanges
NetworkChanges
ResourcePressure
ProcessLifecycle
PowerChanges
```

but underneath each should use the correct native Linux mechanism.

The unification is at the **developer API and policy level**, not necessarily the kernel/event-transport level.

---

# The index can still be the most important Templar authority

I'd give it ownership of a broader concept than simply filename search.

Something like a **persistent storage catalog**:

```text
                    Storage Catalog
                           │
        ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐
        │                  │                  │
   namespace          metadata           generations
        │                  │                  │
 filenames           sizes/type          change history
 parentage           timestamps          freshness
 filesystem ID       allocation          checkpoints
 project ID          attributes
```

Then:

```text
Files
FSearch
Silvaview
Home
project manager
build manager
backup tooling
storage cleaner
recent-files UI
```

can all operate from it.

That's a lot of duplicated scanning eliminated.

But battery, network, GPU, process and memory services don't belong in it.

---

# There's another useful optimization: derived state should have one owner

This generalizes the index idea.

If three programs care about disk free space, don't let all three run:

```text
statvfs()
```

every second.

Have the storage owner maintain that state and signal meaningful changes.

Likewise:

```text
NetworkManager → network truth
UPower         → battery truth
PipeWire       → audio device truth
lege-filed     → filesystem truth
resourced      → workload/resource truth
sessiond       → application/session truth
```

Then consumers don't repeatedly rediscover facts.

This is probably the deeper architectural insight you're reaching:

> **Every piece of derived system state should have one authoritative owner, and all other components should consume its state/events rather than independently measuring the same thing.**

That can materially reduce background work.

---

# I would actually codify this as a TemplarOS design rule

Something along these lines:

### TemplarOS Event-Driven Service Policy

1. **No periodic polling of local state when an event-capable authoritative source exists.**
2. **Each system state domain has one preferred authority.**
3. **Consumers query authorities rather than independently rediscovering state.**
4. **Services should be socket-, D-Bus-, device-, filesystem-, or event-activated where practical.**
5. **Services that do not need persistent in-memory state should terminate when idle.**
6. **File namespace/metadata consumers use the shared storage index, never independent crawlers.**
7. **Resource reactions use kernel threshold events such as PSI rather than high-frequency sampling where possible.**
8. **Timers are reserved for inherently temporal operations, retry/backoff, and bounded reconciliation.**
9. **Polling exceptions must document why no trustworthy event mechanism exists.**
10. **Every persistent daemon must justify why activation-on-demand cannot work.**

That last one is worth emphasizing.

Instead of asking:

> Can we make this daemon lighter?

first ask:

> **Why is this a daemon?**

---

# You could enforce this mechanically

Because you own TemplarOS, this doesn't have to remain a style guide.

Create a service manifest for every Templar component:

```toml
[service]
name = "templar-example"

lifetime = "on-demand"

activation = [
    "dbus:org.templar.Example1"
]

observes = [
    "storage-index"
]

polling = false
```

For a persistent service:

```toml
[service]
name = "lege-filed"

lifetime = "persistent"
justification = """
Owns mount, portal and storage-catalog state requiring
continuous kernel event ownership.
"""
```

And for a legitimate timer:

```toml
[service]
name = "templar-scrub"

lifetime = "oneshot"
activation = ["timer:weekly"]

polling = false
```

Then `xtask`/CI can inspect the installed system for:

* unexpected enabled services;
* unexpected timers;
* Templar services without manifests;
* periodic timers below some threshold;
* services declared persistent without justification.

You can't reliably statically detect every hand-written `sleep(5)` loop in third-party code, but you can absolutely govern **Templar-owned components and systemd units**.

---

## The architecture I'd aim for

```text
                     TemplarOS
                        │
       ┌────────────────┼─────────────────┐
       │                │                 │
   filesystem         devices          network
       │                │                 │
 fsnotify/Btrfs        udev            netlink
       │                │                 │
       ↓                ↓                 ↓
 storage catalog    device owner     NetworkManager
       │                │                 │
       └────────────┬───┴──────────┬──────┘
                    │              │
                   D-Bus / typed APIs
                    │              │
        ┌───────────┴──────────────┴──────────┐
        │                                     │
 on-demand consumers                  persistent core
 start only when needed               only when necessary
        │                                     │
        └──────────── systemd ────────────────┘

 resources:
 kernel PSI/cgroups → resourced

 time:
 systemd timer → oneshot job
```

The target isn't literally **zero background processes**. Some persistent owners are cheaper and more effective than constantly reconstructing their state.

The target is:

> **Zero redundant observation. Zero periodic local-state polling where events exist. Zero permanently resident service that can safely be activation-driven.**

That is both workable and a better formulation of your original idea.

And it could become one of TemplarOS's more meaningful performance differentiators: not merely shipping fewer applications than Lubuntu, but having an explicit OS-wide model in which **state is observed once and reused**, instead of dozens of components independently waking up to ask the machine what changed.

[1]: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/fanotify.7.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com "fanotify(7) - Linux manual page"
[2]: https://dbus.freedesktop.org/doc/dbus-daemon.1.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com "dbus-daemon"
[3]: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/rtnetlink.7.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com "rtnetlink(7) - Linux manual page"
[4]: https://cgit.freedesktop.org/systemd/systemd/tree/src/udev/udevadm-monitor.c?utm_source=chatgpt.com "udevadm-monitor.c « udev « src - systemd/systemd - System and Session Manager"
[5]: https://cdn.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/accounting/psi.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com "PSI - Pressure Stall Information — The Linux Kernel documentation"
